Every projection corner $rDr$ has real rank zero. Moreover, given a projection $r\in D$, a finite set $\mathcal F\subset D$, and $\gamma>0$, there is a finite family of nonzero orthogonal projections $q_1,\ldots,q_m$ with sum $r$ such that $$\label{r:proj:compress-estimate}
  \operatorname{dist}(q_j hq_j,\mathbb Cq_j)<\gamma
  \qquad(h\in\mathcal F,\ 1\le j\le m).$$ The family is empty when $r=0$.

Under the small-full-projection hypothesis of Proposition <a href="#r:proj:split" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="r:proj:split">10</a>, let $q\in D$ be a projection, $s\in[0,1]$, and $\mathcal F\subset D$ a finite set. For every $\varepsilon>0$, there is a projection $e\le q$ such that $$\label{r:proj:tests-estimate}
  |\tau(eh)-s\tau(qh)|<\varepsilon
  \qquad(h\in\mathcal F,\ \tau\in\operatorname T(D)).$$

For projections $z,y\in D$, there is a projection $t\le1-z$ such that $$\begin{aligned}
  \tau(y)-3\tau(yz)&\le\tau(t)\le\tau(y),
       \label{r:proj:move-mass}\\
  \left\|t-y\right\|_{2,\tau}^{2}&\le5\tau(yz)
       \label{r:proj:move-distance}
\end{aligned}$$ for every $\tau\in\operatorname T(D)$. For every contraction $x\in D$, it also satisfies $$\label{r:proj:move-linear}
  |\tau((t-y)x)|
   \le2\sqrt{\tau(y)}\,\left\|t-y\right\|_{2,\tau}.$$

For a finite $K\subseteq\mathcal U(A)$ and $\mu>0$, there are contractions $v_1,\ldots,v_m\in A$ and numbers $\lambda_l\geq0$ with $\sum_l\lambda_l=1$ such that the map $$\Phi(b)=\sum_{l=1}^m\lambda_l v_lbv_l^*,\qquad b\in D,$$ satisfies, for every projection $q\in D$, every $x\in K$, and every $\tau\in\operatorname T(D)$, $$\begin{aligned}
 0\leq\Phi(q)&\leq1,\qquad
 \tau(q)-\mu\leq\tau(\Phi(q))\leq\tau(q),\label{r:eq:diagonal-mass}\\
 \left\|\Phi(q)-x\Phi(q)x^*\right\|_{1,\tau}
 &\leq\mu\tau(q).\label{r:eq:diagonal-central}
\end{aligned}$$

For a bounded sequence $d=(d_j)$ in $B$, with class $[d]\in B^\omega$, one has $$\begin{aligned}
 \sup_{\lambda\in\Lambda_\omega}\lambda([d]^*[d])
 &=\lim_{j\to\omega}\sup_{\tau\in\operatorname T(A)}
             \bar\tau(d_j^*d_j),\label{r:eq:limit-two}\\
 \sup_{\lambda\in\Lambda_\omega}|\lambda([d])|
 &=\lim_{j\to\omega}\sup_{\tau\in\operatorname T(A)}
             |\bar\tau(d_j)|.\label{r:eq:limit-linear}
\end{aligned}$$ In particular, if $\left\|z\right\|_{2,\lambda}=0$ for every $\lambda\in\Lambda_\omega$, then $z=0$ in $B^\omega$.

Fix finitely many variables $x_1,\ldots,x_m$. For each variable prescribe one of the following domains, with a fixed radius $0\le R_i<\infty$: the closed ball of $D$, its selfadjoint part, or its positive part. Let $(P_k)_{k\ge1}$ be countably many $*$-polynomials in these variables with parameters in $D$. For each $k$ choose a test $$t_k(x)=\norm{P_k(x)}_{2,\Lambda}
 \quad\text{or}\quad
 t_k(x)=\sup_{\tau\in\Lambda}|\tau(P_k(x))|,$$ and a number $c_k\ge0$. Suppose that for every $n\ge1$ and every $\varepsilon>0$ there is an admissible tuple $x\in D^m$ with $$t_k(x)<c_k+\varepsilon\qquad(1\le k\le n).$$ Then there is an admissible tuple with $t_k(x)\le c_k$ for every $k$. In particular, when every $c_k=0$, all the prescribed tracial equations hold exactly. A polynomial with zero uniform $2$-norm is zero as an element of $D$.

Let $E\subset A$ be a finite set of selfadjoint contractions and let $0<\eta<1$. There is a selfadjoint $z\in D$ such that, for every $\tau\in\Lambda$, $$\begin{aligned}
  \norm{[z,a]}_{2,\tau}^{2}&\le72\eta &&(a\in E),\\
  \tau(bz)&=0,\qquad \tau(bz^2)=\tau(b) &&(b\in A),\\
  \tau(z^4)&\le3.
 \end{aligned}
 \label{u:eq:averaged-observable}$$ The construction gives $z=\sum_{i=1}^M\sqrt{\beta_i}\,r_i$, where the $r_i$ are selfadjoint unitaries, $\beta_i\ge0$, and $\sum_i\beta_i=1$. In fact, $\tau(z^4)\le3-2\sum_i\beta_i^2$. There is no asserted norm bound on $z$ independent of $M$.

Let $z=z^*\in D$, let $R>0$, and set $$g_R(s)=\max(-R,\min(s,R)),\qquad d=g_R(z).$$ For every $a\in D$ and $\tau\in\Lambda$, $$\norm{[d,a]}_{2,\tau}\le\norm{[z,a]}_{2,\tau}.
 \label{u:eq:clipping-commutator}$$ Moreover, for every contraction $b\in D$, $$\begin{aligned}
  |\tau(b(d-z))|&\le R^{-3}\tau(z^4),\\
  |\tau(b(d^2-z^2))|&\le R^{-2}\tau(z^4),\\
  \tau(d^4)&\le\tau(z^4).
 \end{aligned}
 \label{u:eq:clipping-tails}$$ In particular, if $z$ is obtained from Proposition <a href="#u:prop:averaged-observable" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="u:prop:averaged-observable">33</a>, then $\norm{d}\le R$ and, for every $\tau\in\Lambda$, $$\begin{aligned}
  \norm{[d,a]}_{2,\tau}&\le\sqrt{72\eta} &&(a\in E),\\
  |\tau(bd)|&\le3/R^3 &&(b\in A,\ \norm{b}\le1),\\
  |\tau(b(d^2-1))|&\le3/R^2 &&(b\in A,\ \norm{b}\le1),\\
  \tau(d^4)&\le3
 \end{aligned}
 \label{u:eq:clipped-moments}$$

For every integer $N\geq1$ there exist mutually commuting selfadjoint elements $d_{N,1},\ldots,d_{N,N}\in D\cap A'$, each of norm at most $N$, with the following properties. Put $$C_{N,j}=C^*(A,d_{N,1},\ldots,d_{N,j-1}),
  \qquad
  s_N=\frac{1}{\sqrt N}\sum_{j=1}^N d_{N,j}.$$ For every contraction $b\in C_{N,j}$ and $\tau\in\Lambda$, $$\label{u:eq:row-moments}
  |\tau(bd_{N,j})|\leq\frac{3}{N^3},\qquad
  |\tau(b(d_{N,j}^2-1))|\leq\frac{3}{N^2},\qquad
  \tau(d_{N,j}^4)\leq3.$$ For every fixed $t\in\mathbb R$, $$\label{u:eq:uniform-characteristic-functions}
  \lim_{N\to\infty}
  \sup_{\substack{\tau\in\Lambda\\a\in A,\ \norm{a}\leq1}}
  \left|\tau(ae^{its_N})-e^{-t^2/2}\tau(a)\right|=0.$$

For every fixed positive contraction $a\in A$ and every fixed bounded continuous function $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb C$, $$\label{u:eq:uniform-weak-convergence}
  \lim_{N\to\infty}\sup_{\tau\in\Lambda}
  \left|\tau(af(s_N))-\tau(a)\int_{\mathbb R}f\,d\gamma\right|=0.$$

Let $A$ be a simple, separable, unital, infinite-dimensional nuclear $C^*$-algebra with nonempty tracial-state space and strict comparison as in Definition <a href="#u:def:strict-comparison" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="u:def:strict-comparison">19</a>. For every integer $m\geq1$ there exist pairwise orthogonal projections $p_1,\ldots,p_m\in D\cap A'$ such that $$\label{u:eq:gamma-projections}
  \sum_{i=1}^m p_i=1,
  \qquad
  \tau(ap_i)=\frac{\tau(a)}{m}
  \quad(a\in A,\ \tau\in\Lambda,\ 1\leq i\leq m).$$ In particular, $A$ has uniform property $\Gamma$.

For $g\in A_+$ and $\xi>0$ there is a positive contraction $k$ in a finite matrix algebra over $A$ such that, for every $\sigma\in T$, $$\label{n:eq:rank}
 |\sigma(k)-\sigma(g)|<\xi,\qquad
 0\le d_\sigma(k)-\sigma(k)<\xi.$$

Let $q\in D$ be a projection, $Y\in(qDq)_+$, $0<t<1$, and $\varepsilon>0$. There is a projection $r\le q$ such that, for $\tau\in \Lambda$, $$\label{n:eq:cost-corner}
 \tau(r)=t\tau(q),\qquad
 \tau(rY)\le t\bigl(\tau(Y)+\varepsilon\tau(q)\bigr).$$

If $E\subset D$ is finite dimensional and unital, $Y\in D_+$, $0<t<1$, and $\varepsilon>0$, there is a projection $p\in D\cap E'$ with $$\label{n:eq:cost}
 \tau(pd)=t\tau(d)\quad(d\in E),\qquad
 \tau(pY)\le t\bigl(\tau(Y)+\varepsilon\bigr)\quad(\tau\in \Lambda).$$

Let $0<\lambda<1$, let $\mathcal F$ be a finite set of self-adjoint contractions in $A$, and let $\rho>0$. There is a positive contraction $e\in A$ such that, for $\sigma\in T$ and $x\in\mathcal F$, $$\label{n:eq:small}
 \begin{split}
 \|[e,x]\|_{2,\sigma}&<\rho,\\
 |\sigma(ex)-\lambda\sigma(x)|&<\lambda^{3/2}+\rho,\\
 \sigma(e-e^2)&<\lambda^{3/2}+\rho.
 \end{split}$$

For a finite set $\mathcal F$ of self-adjoint contractions in $A$ and $\Delta>0$, there are unital finite-dimensional subalgebras $D_i\subset D$, self-adjoint contractions $x^{(i)}\in D_i$ for each $x\in\mathcal F$, and positive weights $w_i$ summing to one, such that $$\label{n:eq:models}
 E_i=\sum_{x\in\mathcal F}(x-x^{(i)})^2,
 \qquad \sum_iw_i\tau(E_i)<\Delta\quad(\tau\in \Lambda).$$

Let $p_1,\ldots,p_N$ be projections in a unital $C^*$-algebra with a tracial state $\tau$. Put $$s=\sum_ip_i,\qquad e=\min(s,1),\qquad
 \lambda=\sum_i\tau(p_i),\qquad R=\sum_i\tau\Bigl(p_i\sum_{j<i}p_j\Bigr).$$ For every self-adjoint $x$, $$\label{n:eq:trunc-comm}
 \|[e,x]\|_{2,\tau}
 \le2\Bigl(2\sum_i\|[p_i,x]\|_{2,\tau}^2\Bigr)^{1/2}.$$ Moreover, $$\label{n:eq:trunc-trace}
 \tau(s-e),\ \tau(e-e^2)\le\sqrt{2\lambda R}.$$

For every separable unital $E\subset D$ and $0<\lambda<1$, there is a positive contraction $e\in D\cap E'$ such that, for $\tau\in \Lambda$, $$\label{n:eq:relative-small}
 |\tau(ex)-\lambda\tau(x)|\le\lambda^{3/2}
 \quad(x\in E_{\mathrm{sa}},\ \|x\|\le1),
 \qquad \tau(e-e^2)\le\lambda^{3/2}.$$
